## Coding standards <!-- devready:standards -->

Two tiers, and the tier is the contract. **Enforced** rules are machine-checked by the
gates below — treat them as hard constraints: run the gate before presenting work, fix
what it flags, and never disable, skip, or inline-suppress a check to make a diff pass.
**Advisory** rules are conventions to match when writing new code — follow them in what
you write, but don't refactor passing code just to satisfy them.

### Enforced (machine-checked)
<!-- One line per gate that actually runs, with its command. Fill from
     `devready.sh standards` output; delete layers the repo doesn't have. -->
- Lint: `{{LINT_CMD}}` — zero errors before work is presented
- Format: `{{FORMAT_CMD}}` — every touched file formatted
- Types: `{{TYPECHECK_CMD}}` — must pass
- Tests: `{{TEST_CMD}}` — green before done; new behavior gets a test first

### Advisory (style — match in new code)
<!-- The rules the team wants but no tool checks: naming, error-handling shape,
     comment policy, module boundaries. Keep this list SHORT — if it outgrows the
     enforced list, that's the signal to promote rules into tooling. -->
- {{ADVISORY_RULES}}

A rule this repo doesn't gate is advice, and advice drifts. When an advisory rule gets
argued in review twice, promote it: add the lint rule, hook, or CI step — then move the
line up a tier.
